# The staging build's backend.
#
# This file exists because the APK was built against PRODUCTION.
#
# `.env.local` and `.env.production` both point at
# kartavaya-production.up.railway.app, and neither `vite build` nor
# `npx cap sync` takes a target — so the ordinary local build, the one that
# feeds the Android project, wired the installable app to production. The APK
# verified at 18:04 preconnected to the production host, which is the same host
# it would have called. Twelve real people use that backend, staging and
# production share one Supabase, and a tester tapping through an APK writes
# wherever the bundle points.
#
# Vite resolves `.env.[mode]` ABOVE `.env.local`, so `--mode staging` wins
# without anyone having to remember to edit or delete a file first. That
# ordering is the whole mechanism: `npm run build:staging` cannot silently fall
# back to production.
#
# No secret belongs here. The Supabase anon key is not in the frontend env at
# all — auth goes through the backend — so the only value is a public hostname,
# and the file is committed so the staging target is not one developer's
# untracked local state.
VITE_BACKEND_URL=https://kartavaya-staging.up.railway.app
VITE_ENVIRONMENT=staging
